<section class="section">
  <div class="section-header reveal">
    <h1>Reading Order</h1>
    <p>The Architecture of Survival is an 8-book saga that jumps across 3,200 years of history. Here's every order worth reading it in &mdash; and why each book stands alone.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <p><strong>Read in publication order.</strong> The series deliberately hops between eras &mdash; medieval Germany, the modern day, Bronze Age Egypt &mdash; and publication order reveals its hidden architecture the way it was designed to unfold:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GJTGCHS2">The Aethelred Cipher</a></strong> &mdash; Medieval Germany, 1347. The Black Death, a coded key, a hunted scribe.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GPM973N1">The Genesis Protocol</a></strong> &mdash; Modern day. A geneticist and an engineered bioweapon.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GSWLM1WV">The First Key</a></strong> &mdash; Bronze Age Egypt, 1177 BCE. The origin of everything.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0GX2TG21Y">Love Remembers in Silence</a></strong> &mdash; Roman Judea, 26 CE. Mary of Magdala's buried gospel.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0H34DZVXS">What Augustine Hid</a></strong> &mdash; North Africa, 410 CE. A bishop, a courier, a prophecy of collapse.</li>
      <li><strong><a href="https://www.amazon.com/dp/B0HDK6SGM3">The Split Pomegranate</a></strong> &mdash; Damascus &amp; the Crusades, 1140&ndash;1187. A love in coded seals.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Books 7 (the eugenics era, 1933&ndash;1953) and 8 (the finale, 2038&ndash;2100) are in development.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>Do I have to read them in order?</h2>
    <p>No. <strong>Every book stands alone</strong> &mdash; a complete story, in its own era, with its own protagonist. The connections reward series readers but are never required. Three good entry doors:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Love medieval mysteries?</strong> Start with Book 1, <em>The Aethelred Cipher</em> &mdash; for readers of <em>The Name of the Rose</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Prefer modern thrillers?</strong> Start with Book 2, <em>The Genesis Protocol</em> &mdash; Crichton-style science and conspiracy.</li>
      <li><strong>Want the chronological beginning?</strong> Start with Book 3, <em>The First Key</em> &mdash; the Bronze Age origin story.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="concept-section reveal">
    <h2>Chronological order (by historical era)</h2>
    <p>For readers who want to travel time in a straight line: The First Key (1177 BCE) &rarr; Love Remembers in Silence (26 CE) &rarr; What Augustine Hid (410 CE) &rarr; The Split Pomegranate (1140 CE) &rarr; The Aethelred Cipher (1347 CE) &rarr; The Genesis Protocol (today). It works &mdash; but you'll meet some revelations before the books that set them up.</p>
  </div>

